p.src:
Acceleration is the rate of change of velocity and hence the second time derivative of position: $\mathbf a(t)=\frac{d\mathbf v}{dt}
  =\dot{\mathbf v}(t)
  =\frac{d^2\mathbf r}{dt^2}
  =\ddot{\mathbf r}(t),
  \qquad
  a_i=\ddot x_i.$ Acceleration is also a vector. If a particle moving in the positive $x$-direction speeds up, $a_x>0$. If it slows down, $a_x<0$. What is often called deceleration is simply acceleration opposite to the current velocity. More generally, velocity can change by changing its direction even when its magnitude remains constant.

